  "response": "## First, a command correction\n\n`crimes scan` takes a directory — `crimes scan src/date.ts` exits with `crimes: not a directory`. The per-file command is `crimes context`:\n\n```\nnode packages/cli/dist/index.js context evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/date.ts\n```\n\nThe JSON in the prompt is a repo-wide `scan` (42 findings across 21 files). The DST finding is `crime_00017`, fingerprint `dst_naive_arithmetic::src/date.ts::`. I re-ran `context` on the file to confirm it against the source.\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\nThe finding is real, and the source confirms it — `src/date.ts:51-52`:\n\n```ts\nexport function tomorrowMs(nowMs: number): number {\n  return nowMs + 86400000;\n}\n```\n\nThe evidence, verbatim from the finding:\n\n> `+ 86400000 (day) @L52`\n> `lines: 52`\n> `use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math`\n\nand the summary:\n\n> `1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.`\n\n**Why request changes and not block:**\n\n- `severity: \"medium\"`, `confidence: 0.8` — the detector's own calibration doesn't put this at blocker grade.\n- `\"blast_radius\": 0`, `\"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 0` — the `context` readout renders this as *\"blast bottom-quartile (0 direct importers)\"*. Nothing imports `tomorrowMs` yet. This is the cheapest moment in its life to fix it; it is also the moment it can hurt the fewest callers.\n- `\"effort\": \"medium\"`, `suggested_actions[0].risk: \"low\"` — a low-risk fix, so there's no \"we'll do it later\" argument to concede to.\n\n**Why not wave it through, either.** `crimes context` puts the file at `risk: HIGH (6 findings: 2 high, 2 medium, 2 low)`. This PR adds a seventh temporal hazard to a file that already carries `Half-UTC, Half-Local` (severity `high`) with evidence *\"`\"d\"` uses `getUTCFullYear()` @L9 and `getMonth()` @L41\"*. `tomorrowMs` takes `number` and returns `number` — the timezone that decides whether the answer is right doesn't appear in the signature at all. Compose it with `todayKey()` (UTC) or `dateLabel()` (mixed) and the zone mismatch is silent. That's the agent-risk argument, and it's the one I'd actually write in the PR comment.\n\n**One honest caveat on the evidence.** `+ 86400000 (day) @L52` is an AST fact: it proves the constant is used in day arithmetic, not that the result is consumed as a *wall-clock day*. If `tomorrowMs` only ever feeds an absolute 24-hour TTL comparison (`isExpired`-style), adding 86.4M ms is exactly correct and this is a false positive worth recording:\n\n```\ncrimes feedback dst_naive_arithmetic::src/date.ts:: --verdict fp --note \"...\"\n```\n\nHere the author's own comment at `src/date.ts:49-50` settles it — *\"'+ 1 day' using a raw ms constant\"* — so it's a true positive.\n\n## The two dates per year\n\nThey are the two **DST transitions in the host's local timezone** — which means this is zone-dependent, and a host running `TZ=UTC` never hits it at all. For 2026:\n\n| Zone | Spring forward (23-hour day) | Fall back (25-hour day) |\n|---|---|---|\n| US (`America/New_York`) | **Sun 8 March 2026**, 02:00 → 03:00 | **Sun 1 November 2026**, 02:00 → 01:00 |\n| UK/EU (`Europe/London`) | **Sun 29 March 2026**, 01:00 → 02:00 | **Sun 25 October 2026**, 02:00 → 01:00 |\n\nThe error fires when the 24-hour absolute window *straddles* the transition instant, so it's really the transition day plus the tail of the day before. Both directions produce an off-by-one-**calendar-day** answer, not just an off-by-one-hour one:\n\n**Fall back** — the local day is 25 hours, so +86,400,000 ms lands an hour *short* of the same wall-clock time tomorrow:\n\n```\ntomorrowMs(2026-11-01T00:30 EDT)  →  2026-11-01T23:30 EST\nexpected 2026-11-02 · got 2026-11-01 — \"tomorrow\" is still today\n```\n\n**Spring forward** — the local day is 23 hours, so it lands an hour *past*, jumping a date boundary early:\n\n```\ntomorrowMs(2026-03-07T23:30 EST)  →  2026-03-09T00:30 EDT\nexpected 2026-03-08 · got 2026-03-09 — a day is skipped entirely\n```\n\n## What I'd ask for in the PR\n\nPer `suggested_actions[0]`: *\"Replace ms-arithmetic with a timezone-aware addDays/subtract operation that knows about DST and leap years.\"* Concretely — `Temporal.ZonedDateTime.prototype.add({ days: 1 })` or Luxon's `.plus({ days: 1 })`, with the zone as an explicit parameter rather than an ambient host default.\n\nPlus two regression tests pinned at those transition instants. The file has none today: `\"test_gap\": 0.5`, and `context` reports *\"no test file matched the target basename\"*. A DST bug with no test is a bug that comes back.\n\n(Minor nit on the detector copy: the \"and leap years\" clause doesn't apply to *this* finding — leap days are whole days, so an 86.4M-ms constant handles them fine. It's the `+30 days`/`+1 year` variants of ms math where leap years bite.)",
